%erg v1
Title: Design cross-repo blocker references and erg graph visualization
Status: open
Created: 2026-05-02
Author: claude

--- log ---
2026-05-02T00:00Z claude created — design ticket; spec gap: Blocked-by: gh#N is single-repo only

--- body ---
## Problem

`Blocked-by: gh#N` (rules/tickets.md:69-74) resolves N against the
*current* repo only. Real coordination crosses repos: a ticket in
`git-erg` may legitimately wait on an issue in another repo
(tooling, upstream library, sibling service). Today the only
encodings are:

- Drop the dependency from the ticket entirely → invisible blocker.
- Encode it in prose in the body → unparseable, invisible to
  `erg ready` and `erg graph`.

Neither is acceptable for a system whose value proposition is
machine-readable dependency state.

## Proposal — syntax

Extend `Blocked-by` to accept a fully-qualified GitHub reference:

```
Blocked-by: gh#owner/repo#N      # cross-repo issue
Blocked-by: gh#N                  # same-repo issue (unchanged)
Blocked-by: 0042                  # local ticket (unchanged)
```

Grammar (additive, v1-compatible):

```
ref       := local-ref | gh-ref
local-ref := [0-9]{4}
gh-ref    := "gh#" [ owner "/" repo "#" ] number
owner     := [A-Za-z0-9_.-]+ ; matches GitHub login rules
repo      := [A-Za-z0-9_.-]+
number    := [1-9][0-9]*
```

Bare `gh#N` continues to mean "this repo" (no migration cost).

## Resolution semantics

| Ref kind | Online | Offline |
|---|---|---|
| Local `NNNN` | filesystem check | filesystem check |
| `gh#N` | GitHub API → state | treated as satisfied |
| `gh#owner/repo#N` | GitHub API → state | treated as satisfied |

Offline-tolerance matches the existing rule (rules/tickets.md:73-74).
A blocker is "satisfied" when the issue/PR is `closed` *and merged*
for PRs, or simply `closed` for issues. (Same as today; restated for
the cross-repo case so we don't accidentally unblock on a closed-as-
not-planned upstream.)

## Validator changes (tickets/tools/go)

1. Parse the extended grammar in the `Blocked-by` header.
2. Reject malformed refs (e.g. `gh#owner/repo` with no number,
   `gh#/repo#1`, mixed-case schemes other than the literal `gh#`).
3. Local-ref existence check unchanged.
4. **Do not** validate cross-repo refs against the network — keep
   the validator hermetic. Network resolution lives in
   `erg ready`/`erg graph`, not in `erg validate`.
5. Cycle detection: cross-repo refs cannot participate in local
   cycles by construction (they're terminal from this repo's view),
   so the existing DAG check is unaffected.

## `erg ready` changes

- Each `gh#owner/repo#N` is resolved via API when online; treated
  as satisfied when offline (existing rule).
- API call is a single `GET /repos/{owner}/{repo}/issues/{N}`; cache
  per-invocation only (no on-disk cache — keeps the system stateless,
  matches the "never caches" rule in rules/tickets.md:191).
- Rate-limit handling: on 403/429, treat the blocker as **unknown**
  and exclude the ticket from ready (fail-closed, surfaces the
  problem). Print a one-line warning to stderr.

## `erg graph` changes — visualization

The DAG renderer (0004) currently shows local edges. Cross-repo
edges need a distinct visual language so a reader can tell at a
glance "this is leaving the repo".

ASCII output:

```
0016 cross-repo blocker design [open, READY]
0021 ship feature X [open, blocked]
  -> gh#anthropics/claude-code#1234 [closed]   (cross-repo, satisfied)
  -> gh#42 [open]                              (same-repo)
  -> 0019 internal prep [closed]
```

Conventions:
- Cross-repo refs render as `gh#owner/repo#N` verbatim (no
  truncation — the owner/repo *is* the information).
- Annotation column shows resolved state in brackets, plus a
  parenthetical kind tag (`cross-repo`, `same-repo`).
- Offline mode renders `[unknown, offline]` instead of guessing.

JSON output (`erg graph --json`):

```json
{
  "id": "0021",
  "blocked_by": [
    {"kind": "gh-cross", "owner": "anthropics", "repo": "claude-code",
     "number": 1234, "state": "closed", "satisfied": true},
    {"kind": "gh-same",  "number": 42, "state": "open", "satisfied": false},
    {"kind": "local",    "id": "0019", "state": "closed", "satisfied": true}
  ]
}
```

The `kind` discriminator lets downstream tools distinguish without
re-parsing the ref string.

## Open questions

1. **Authentication.** API calls need a token for private repos
   and to escape the 60/hr unauthenticated limit. Reuse `gh auth
   token` if available; fall back to `GITHUB_TOKEN`; else
   unauthenticated. Document this in `rules/tickets.md`.
2. **PRs vs issues.** GitHub treats PRs as issues at the API level,
   but "closed" on a PR can mean merged or rejected. We should
   require `merged_at != null` for PR refs to be satisfied. Worth
   spelling out in the spec.
3. **Other forges.** `gh#` is GitHub-specific. If/when we need
   GitLab, the natural extension is `gl#owner/repo#N`. This ticket
   does NOT add `gl#` — only reserves the namespace shape so a
   future `gl#` doesn't require re-design.
4. **Visualization beyond ASCII.** Should `erg graph` grow a
   `--dot` Graphviz output? Out of scope here; file a follow-up if
   the ASCII view proves insufficient for cross-repo-heavy graphs.

## Sequencing

- Independent of 0012 (Status drop) and 0015 (Tags) — touches a
  different header.
- Should land after 0014 (modularize Go source) so the resolver
  has a clean home (likely `tickets/tools/go/internal/resolver/`).
- Visualization piece extends 0004 (already shipped).

## Exit criteria

- [ ] `rules/tickets.md` documents extended `Blocked-by` grammar
      and resolution semantics
- [ ] Validator accepts `gh#owner/repo#N`, rejects malformed forms
- [ ] `Erg` struct exposes parsed refs with kind discriminator
- [ ] `erg ready` resolves cross-repo refs online, treats as
      satisfied offline, fail-closed on rate-limit
- [ ] `erg graph` ASCII renders cross-repo edges with kind tag
      and resolved state
- [ ] `erg graph --json` emits structured ref objects with `kind`
- [ ] Tests cover: valid cross-repo ref, malformed ref rejected,
      offline behavior, rate-limit fail-closed, graph rendering
      round-trip
- [ ] Open questions 1 and 2 resolved in spec text before merge
